{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Pipeline/TaskGraph/MANUAL_DELIVERY_RECORD_TEMPLATE.md'","aggregated_output":"# Phase 1 Manual Delivery Record Template\n\n> Transitional audit record only. Completing this form does not automatically authorize a task or mark it complete. Phase 2 will replace this with a deterministic evidence/conformance schema.\n\n## Task contract\n\n- Task ID:\n- Task title:\n- Task file:\n- Task file SHA-256:\n- Governing GDD commit:\n- Governing GDD section(s):\n- Human-approved clarifications/decisions:\n\n## Frozen implementation inputs\n\n- Base branch:\n- Base commit:\n- Working branch:\n- Worker/model:\n- Start time:\n- End time:\n\n## Implementation result\n\n- Candidate commit:\n- Changed files:\n- New files:\n- Deleted files:\n- Scope deviations:\n- Newly discovered prerequisite work:\n\n## Deterministic and Unity evidence\n\n- Git/scope checks:\n- Static checks:\n- Unity version:\n- Unity compilation result:\n- EditMode test command/result/evidence path:\n- PlayMode test command/result/evidence path:\n- Build command/result/evidence path:\n- Runtime observations:\n- Known warnings or failures:\n\n## Human validation\n\n- Required human checks:\n- Person performing checks:\n- Result:\n- Notes:\n- Unresolved concerns:\n\n## Integration\n\n- Merge/approval result:\n- Integrated commit:\n- Integrated tree:\n- Post-merge validation performed:\n- Post-merge validation result:\n\n## Throughput and intervention\n\n- Human orchestration time:\n- Agent execution time:\n- Repair attempts:\n- Interruptions/recoveries:\n- Manual code/editor changes required:\n- What should be automated next:\n- What should remain human-owned:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Pipeline/TaskGraph/CONFORMANCE_RECORDS.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Pipeline/TaskGraph/CONFORMANCE_RECORDS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Delivery/Baseline/Revalidation Evidence Records\n\nPhase 3A derives the current conformance of one schema-v2 task from immutable, committed evidence. Records are historical facts; they never contain mutable readiness or completion authority.\n\n## Locations\n\n```text\nPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/<TASK-ID>/records/<RECORD-ID>.json\nPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/<TASK-ID>/artifacts/<files>\n```\n\nOnly files committed at `HEAD` are read. Uncommitted records, contracts, canon, and artifacts cannot affect the result.\n\n## Common schema 1.0\n\nEvery record has exactly these common fields plus exactly one of `delivery`, `baseline`, or `revalidation`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"record_type\": \"delivery\",\n  \"record_id\": \"DEL-NSC-003-EXAMPLE\",\n  \"task_id\": \"NSC-003\",\n  \"task_contract\": {\n    \"path\": \"Tasks/NSC-003.yaml\",\n    \"revision\": 1,\n    \"sha256\": \"semantic canonical JSON SHA-256\"\n  },\n  \"canon\": {\n    \"path\": \"Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md\",\n    \"sha256\": \"normalized UTF-8 text SHA-256\"\n  },\n  \"validated_state\": {\"commit\": \"Git commit SHA\", \"tree\": \"Git tree SHA\"},\n  \"conformance_surfaces\": [\n    {\"path\": \"repository/path\", \"blob_sha\": \"Git blob SHA\", \"role\": \"implementation\"}\n  ],\n  \"gate_results\": [\n    {\n      \"gate_id\": \"VAL-001\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"path\": \"Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/NSC-003/artifacts/result.txt\",\n          \"blob_sha\": \"Git blob SHA\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"notes\": \"\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"human_approval\": {\n    \"required\": false,\n    \"decision\": \"not_required\",\n    \"approved_by\": \"\",\n    \"notes\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"recorded_at\": \"2026-08-22T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nTask-contract hashes parse the JSON-compatible YAML and hash UTF-8 canonical JSON with sorted keys and compact separators. Canon hashes tolerate a UTF-8 BOM and normalize CRLF and lone CR to LF. Surfaces and gate artifacts use Git blob SHAs.\n\nDelivery records add:\n\n```json\n\"delivery\": {\n  \"base_commit\": \"...\",\n  \"candidate_commit\": \"...\",\n  \"integrated_commit\": \"...\",\n  \"integrated_tree\": \"...\"\n}\n```\n\n`validated_state` must exactly equal the integrated commit/tree.\n\nBaseline records establish the first trustworthy evidence state for an implementation that existed before this evidence system. They do not claim when that implementation was authored or delivered. Their IDs use the `BASE-` prefix, and they add:\n\n```json\n\"baseline\": {\n  \"reason_type\": \"pre_evidence_existing_implementation\",\n  \"summary\": \"Why this existing implementation is being baselined\"\n}\n```\n\nThe summary must be non-empty. The validated commit/tree is the actual integrated state tested. Baselines contain no `base_commit`, `candidate_commit`, or `integrated_commit` fields. A valid baseline establishes conformant state exactly as a valid delivery does.\n\nRevalidation records add:\n\n```json\n\"revalidation\": {\n  \"basis_record_id\": \"DEL-NSC-003-EXAMPLE\",\n  \"reason_type\": \"code_change\",\n  \"summary\": \"Why the earlier evidence was revalidated\"\n}\n```\n\nAllowed reasons are `code_change`, `gdd_change`, `contract_change`, `periodic`, and `manual`. The basis may be a delivery, baseline, or prior revalidation record. It must be a same-task committed record, basis chains must be acyclic, and the basis validated commit must be an ancestor of the revalidation commit.\n\n## Validation and selection\n\nThe evaluator rejects absolute/non-canonical/traversing paths, unsupported schema fields, mutable authority fields (`status`, `complete`, `current`, `ready`, `authorized`), identity/path disagreement, duplicate IDs, duplicate gate IDs, duplicate surface paths, false trees/hashes/blobs, and incomplete current gate sets.\n\nEvery recorded gate must be `pass`. Required human approval establishes conformance only with `decision: approved` and a non-empty `approved_by`; otherwise the derived state is `needs_human`.\n\nWhen multiple records are current-valid, a record at a strict descendant validated commit supersedes ancestors. Multiple maximal records that cannot be reduced to one by commit ancestry produce `ambiguous_evidence`; timestamps never break ties.\n\nDerived state precedence is:\n\n1. `cancelled` / `superseded`\n2. `aggregate`\n3. `invalid_evidence` / `ambiguous_evidence`\n4. `conformant`\n5. `needs_replan`\n6. `needs_human`\n7. `needs_revalidation`\n8. `not_delivered`\n\n## Commands\n\n```text\npython3 Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py state NSC-003\npython3 Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py state NSC-003 --json\npython3 Pipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_evaluator_smoke_test.py\n```\n\nPhase 3A enables evidence-derived current-state inspection through `taskcontrol state`. Baseline evidence is immutable history, never mutable current/completion/readiness authority.\n\nNSC-023 is the first real production baseline example. At committed HEAD, `BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5` proves the current state of the Fixed Isometric Camera as `conformant`. Its validated implementation is commit `86af98f41ab53016ef55eca9516cc339a1e4f5d1`, tree `3e89c4a4879d1bf4179ae48f95b85dee1abc0d4d`, and its evidence was committed in `8933e67c7767abf45634f7bade79c734f334eea5`. Uncommitted evidence was correctly ignored before that evidence commit.\n\nNo real production revalidation record exists yet. Do not fabricate a gameplay, contract, GDD, or implementation change merely to produce one; the first legitimate relevant change will exercise production revalidation.\n\nA conformant result does not establish dependency readiness. Dependency-readiness policy and dispatch authorization policy have not been implemented or approved: `taskcontrol ready` remains unavailable, `taskcontrol authorize` remains denied with exit code `2` and reason code `evidence_derived_dispatch_policy_not_enabled`, and zero tasks may be autonomously dispatched. State inspection and a conformant result never authorize autonomous execution.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-033_EVIDENCE_DERIVED_CONFORMANCE.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-033_EVIDENCE_DERIVED_CONFORMANCE.md'","aggregated_output":"# ADR-033: Evidence-Derived Current Conformance\n\n- Status: Accepted\n- Date: 2026-08-22\n- Scope: Architecture Correction Phase 3A\n\n## Context\n\nSchema-v2 task contracts define approved work but deliberately contain no operational completion truth. A historical delivery can cease to conform when its contract, governing GDD, implementation surfaces, or evidence artifacts change. Working-copy files also cannot be repository authority because they are neither immutable nor integrated.\n\n## Decision\n\nIntroduce minimal immutable delivery, baseline, and revalidation records under `Pipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/<TASK-ID>/`. Evaluate current conformance exclusively from committed `HEAD` and referenced Git objects. A baseline establishes first trustworthy evidence for an implementation that predates the evidence system without claiming its original authorship or delivery date.\n\nEach record binds an exact semantic task-contract hash and revision, normalized canonical-GDD hash, validated commit/tree, conformance-surface blob set, exact completion-gate result set, committed evidence-artifact blobs, and any required human approval. Delivery records bind the validated state to the integrated state. Baseline records identify the actual integrated state tested without inventing delivery commits and can establish conformance exactly as delivery records can. Revalidation records form an acyclic, same-task ancestry chain from a delivery, baseline, or prior revalidation record.\n\nCurrent conformance is derived, never written. A later unrelated commit preserves conformance when all bound surfaces and canon remain unchanged. Contract changes require replanning; canon or tracked-surface changes require revalidation. Invalid or contradictory evidence fails closed.\n\nWhen several records remain valid, Git commit ancestry selects a unique strict descendant. Incomparable maximal records are ambiguous. `recorded_at` is audit metadata and never selection authority.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Evaluation is repeatable for a given committed `HEAD` and does not depend on working-copy formatting or timestamps.\n- Evidence is auditable through native Git commits, trees, and blobs.\n- Record creation remains manual in Phase 3A; no production evidence record, including for NSC-023, is introduced.\n- Evidence-derived current-state inspection exists, but production delivery/baseline/revalidation evidence has not yet been proven on a real task.\n- Dependency-readiness and dispatch authorization policy remain outside this decision and are not enabled. Readiness is not derived, authorization remains denied, and zero tasks may be autonomously dispatched.\n- State inspection alone, including a `conformant` result, never authorizes execution.\n- Claiming, attempts, and supervision remain outside this decision and stay disabled.\n- GDDRAG and its index are unchanged; the evaluator reads the canonical GDD directly from Git.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Pipeline/AgentRuntime/README.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'git show 9f2485dca1b61360e6d02810adc71e021346496e:Pipeline/AgentRuntime/README.md'","aggregated_output":"# Provider-neutral AgentRuntime\n\nThis runtime foundation extracts the reusable orchestration ideas demonstrated by Assignment 3: fresh bounded invocations, explicit request/result handoffs, semantic capability limits, structured output, normalized failures, and immutable run artifacts. `AgentCrew/` and `Assignment6GER/` remain unchanged historical coursework evidence; this package neither imports nor retrofits them.\n\n`AgentInvocationRequest` describes one generic bounded invocation: run identity, role, prompt, repository context, semantic capabilities, write policy, output shape, capability-class selection, budgets, and provider configuration. It has no No Safe Circle task identity and no dependency on `Tasks/*.yaml`. `AgentResult` describes only an invocation outcome and provider/agent claims. Task identity belongs to `Pipeline/TaskExecution`; delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, integration, repository changes, command execution, and test success remain decisions for higher deterministic repository, test, and evidence systems.\n\nAll retained request, result, and runtime-configuration JSON values are recursively validated and frozen. Runtime configuration invariants apply equally to direct construction, dictionaries, and strict JSON loading. Only exact built-in JSON null, booleans, integers, finite numbers, valid UTF-8 strings, lists/tuples, dictionaries, and the runtime's own frozen mapping proxies are accepted. Polymorphic container and text subclasses are rejected so provider callbacks cannot run during validation or make publication disagree with the validated snapshot. Cycles and nesting deeper than the conservative 64-container limit are rejected deterministically. `to_dict()` methods return detached mutable copies. The runner accepts only the exact `AgentInvocationRequest`, `RuntimeConfiguration`, and `ProviderInvocationResponse` types; requests additionally require exact `WriteBoundaries` and `Budgets`, while results require exact `Usage`. Base serializers are called non-polymorphically, preventing subclass serialization or response-container overrides at these trust boundaries.\n\nIdentifiers use conservative lowercase ASCII forms: run IDs are 1–64 character lowercase alphanumeric/hyphen slugs, while roles and configuration keys begin with a lowercase letter and use lowercase letters, digits, underscores, and separated hyphens. Every serialized operational string must be UTF-8 encodable. Repository paths are already-normalized, relative POSIX paths and additionally reject surrogates, NUL, ASCII controls, DEL, line breaks, tabs, Windows-forbidden characters, alternate-data-stream colons, trailing dots/spaces, and reserved Windows device names. Write-boundary components are compared conservatively and case-insensitively while remaining component-aware; denied boundaries override allowed ones. Budget maxima are 1,000 turns, 86,400 seconds, and 10,000,000 tokens.\n\nProvider adapters expose an identifier, accept the common request plus a configured concrete model, return structured output and raw log text separately, and normalize provider failures. They translate invocation mechanics only. They may never write task/evidence/readiness state or declare delivery, conformance, readiness, or authorization. Live-provider adapters must pass the same contract suite.\n\n`ProviderOutputInvalid` represents provider output from which an adapter could not produce a structured-output candidate. `AgentRunner` normalizes it to `schema_error`. This is distinct from a successfully parsed candidate that the runner later rejects through central schema validation, although both outcomes normalize to `schema_error`. `ProviderRequestRejected` represents a provider that cannot safely honor a selected request policy and normalizes to `invalid_request` after provider/model selection. Exact provider raw output is retained in `provider.log` when it is valid. Neither exception establishes deterministic correctness, delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, approval, integration, or test-success authority.\n\n`FakeProvider` is deterministic and performs no network, command, version-control, engine, or repository access. It exercises success, provider failure, invalid provider output, timeout, permission denial, budget exhaustion, and centrally detected schema failure.\n\n`ClaudeCodeProvider` now includes **Stage 4B.2 — Practical Repository Read/Search**. It supports the empty capability set, `repository_read`, `repository_search`, and their combination. Empty-capability invocations retain the Stage 4B behavior: a new empty temporary directory outside the repository with no tools. Repository-capable invocations run against the actual source repository root (`/workspace` in Docker): `repository_read` exposes native `Read`, while `repository_search` exposes native `Glob` and `Grep`. Repository write, approved command execution, and every non-null token limit remain rejected before launch.\n\nNonempty `context_paths` remain invalid without a repository capability. With a repository capability they are accepted as validated repository-relative invocation hints and added to a generated repository-context section in the effective provider prompt without mutating the `AgentInvocationRequest`. They are guidance, not a hardened read allowlist. This trusted single-user boundary intentionally prioritizes useful access to the current working tree; ADR-038 records the accepted limited risk.\n\nClaude receives the exact concrete configured model with no alias or fallback, native `--max-turns` equal to the request's required positive `turn_limit`, and the independent external hard `timeout_seconds` enforced by the shared subprocess transport. The prompt travels through stdin, while the compact output schema, exact `--tools` value, and explicit `Bash`, `Edit`, `Write`, `NotebookEdit`, `WebSearch`, and `WebFetch` denial travel as direct argv elements with `shell=False`; no `--allowedTools` argument is emitted. The shared standard-library transport captures stdout and stderr concurrently through `communicate`, creates a new process group/session, terminates the group on timeout with bounded cleanup, and preserves available partial output. Deterministic adapter and AgentRunner integration tests inject a fake process runner and never launch Claude. The opt-in `tests/claude_repository_read_search_live_smoke.py` requires `NSC_RUN_CLAUDE_REPOSITORY_SMOKE=1`; normal deterministic runs skip live Claude access.\n\nClaude stdout is decoded strictly as UTF-8 and retained exactly as `provider.log`. A successful response must be one JSON result envelope with exact built-in status metadata: `type=result`, `is_error=false`, `subtype=success`, `terminal_reason=completed`, and a present `structured_output`; `stop_reason=tool_use` is acceptable. Missing or malformed status or permission-denial metadata is `ProviderOutputInvalid`. A nonempty, exact-list `permission_denials`, other well-formed unsuccessful result envelopes, and nonzero exits are provider failures. `ProviderTransportError` covers local process/transcript failures, invalid usage metadata, and unexpected stderr on a zero exit, and `AgentRunner` normalizes it to `internal_error` while retaining any valid exact raw log. Timeouts remain `timeout` and retain valid partial stdout.\n\nNormalized Claude usage prefers all `modelUsage` entries, including cache-read and cache-creation input tokens, and otherwise falls back conservatively to top-level `usage`. An explicitly present empty `modelUsage` object and malformed, negative, boolean, non-finite, or inconsistent usage fail closed as transport errors.\n\nEach caller-supplied run root receives a uniquely and atomically created `<run_id>/` containing canonical `request.json`, `provider.log`, and `result.json`. JSON artifacts use strict finite JSON, deterministic key ordering, UTF-8 bytes, LF line endings, and one trailing LF. `provider.log` is the provider's exact raw text and is not decorated with runtime diagnostics. Each file is written and fsynced as a temporary file, then atomically hard-linked into place only if its destination does not exist; failed publication cleans its temporary file and cannot overwrite existing bytes. Existing run directories are never reused. A run directory is an audit artifact, not evidence of correctness.\n\nAfter a valid request exists, the runner creates the run directory and publishes `request.json` before resolving provider configuration. Unknown configurations, unavailable configured providers, and missing capability mappings therefore produce a preserved failed `result.json` classified as `invalid_request` without invoking a provider. Provider and model are null only when this pre-invocation selection did not succeed; post-selection failures retain the selected values.\n\nEvery failed `AgentResult` has a non-whitespace, non-authoritative `failure_message` and must keep `structured_output` null so rejected output cannot appear accepted; successful results leave the diagnostic null or empty. This diagnostic preserves configuration, provider-exception, schema-validation, and rejected-metadata details. It never establishes delivery, conformance, readiness, authorization, test success, or approval. Provider invocation failures and structured-output validation are normalized separately: unexpected ordinary provider exceptions are `internal_error`, while invalid structured JSON or schema mismatch is `schema_error`. Provider responses are field-snapshotted exactly once after their exact-type check. Structured output is immediately detached and frozen, and that same snapshot is both schema-validated and retained. Valid usage and execution/change/test claims survive a post-invocation `schema_error`, while rejected structured output remains null. Invalid raw logs are replaced by an empty log and a safe diagnostic; malformed exception text cannot block artifact publication. Process-control exceptions are not swallowed.\n\nStructured output supports only these JSON Schema features: the object, array, string, integer, number, boolean, and null types; `properties`; `required`; boolean `additionalProperties`; `items`; `enum`; and finite numeric `minimum`/`maximum` on integer or number schemas. Unknown keywords fail closed. The runner—not an adapter—validates successful output.\n\n`OpenAICodexProvider` supports empty capability, repository read, repository search, and combined read/search requests. Repository access requires an explicit externally enforced read-only construction profile; empty-capability requests use a temporary directory outside the repository. It forwards the selected concrete model, sends prompts by stdin, preserves exact JSONL stdout, parses the final-output file, uses the last completed-turn usage event, and enforces `min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)`. ArchitectureReview supplies the external Docker read-only profile. Repository writes, approved command execution, non-null token limits, fallback, and production role orchestration remain unsupported.\n\nThe runtime still has no crew roles/prompts, engine execution, GER integration, task selection, version-control workflow, TaskGraph evidence creation, conformance/readiness derivation, or dispatch authorization.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
